# WolkaraForever layer patch 0904 - the Metal trace records render-target sizes, and can be turned on from a file
#
# Why: the game's own log ("Render Settings Changed ... Size: WxH(window @ scale%)") is the only
# record of the resolution it draws its 3D world at, and on the iPhone Duo its numbers do not
# follow the Render Scale in a straight line (50% logged 475x334, 71.3% logged 1334x939, of a
# 2669x1878 window). The textures the game asks Metal for are the direct answer.
#
# What changes, only while the trace is on (it stays off by default):
#   - a texture created for rendering into is logged with its width and height, once per
#     distinct kind and size, so a change of Render Scale shows up as new sizes;
#   - every trace line carries the time of day, to line it up with the game's log;
#   - besides the launch argument --trace-metal, a file named trace-metal in the app's Documents
#     folder turns the trace on, because a phone installed from Linux is not started with launch
#     arguments. Delete the file to turn it off again.
# Nothing the game creates is changed: each call still goes to the original method.
#
# Applies to: translation/Metal/MetalTrace.m
# Upstream: candidate (diagnostics only).
#
diff --git a/translation/Metal/MetalTrace.m b/translation/Metal/MetalTrace.m
--- a/translation/Metal/MetalTrace.m
+++ b/translation/Metal/MetalTrace.m
@@ -1,5 +1,7 @@
-// Development diagnostics, opt-in (launch argument --trace-metal): what the
-// application asks Metal to create, and above all what iPadOS refuses.
+// Development diagnostics, opt-in (launch argument --trace-metal, or a file named
+// trace-metal in the app's Documents folder, for devices where launch arguments
+// cannot be given): what the application asks Metal to create, and above all what
+// iPadOS refuses.
 //
 // Every failed creation is logged with its description: a texture, buffer or
 // heap that comes back nil, a library compiled from source that does not
@@ -10,12 +12,21 @@
 // so a run shows which macOS habits (Managed storage, linear textures in
 // buffers, tile pipelines) the application relies on. Nothing is changed: each
 // call goes to the original method with the original arguments.
+//
+// Render targets are also logged once per distinct size, with the time of day:
+// they are the only direct record of the resolution the game draws its 3D world
+// at (its Render Scale), as opposed to the window it presents.
